# Provenance of this corpus.
#
# Update this file each time you repin the plugin, so that the next repin can
# diff tag to tag.

repo     https://github.com/lukewilliamboswell/roc-ray
tag      0.11.1
platform 4NWnSt7bKCyVx4xDFH89pV11ekYbiGQ43kdjfZ9NBJQn.tar.zst
# The release's other bundle, 4HTCkB6nkWxGHMP3aMvifD5a9CfyNfVGsD5cEpZURxcf.tar.zst,
# is the Wayland build of the same platform. Its .roc modules have the same API.
# Nothing here pins it. There is no types bundle. The platform contains the
# roc-ray-types package of the release candidates, so this plugin has no types/
# directory and the manifest has no `packages` entry.
#
# 0.11.1 republishes 0.11.0. The platform is unchanged, and only the examples
# archive differs: 0.11.0's entries had no file type, and the nightly runner
# refused the archive. This plugin took its examples from 0.11.1's archive.
# 0.11.0 bundle: JoBzGMU8iK4WzhkVGUjrDvfev2TTjYPhQfPphEVFeux.tar.zst. This
# plugin does not pin it.

# The manifest's `compiler` line is the nightly the release says it was
# "Built and tested with": nightly-2026-10-23-18478ff. The release notes add
# that the platform names no compiler: neither its bundle header nor any
# example has a `roc:` entry, so a compiler pin is not part of the release.
# Every app here was checked under nightly-2026-10-23-18478ff, which is not
# the nightly in corpus/language/UPSTREAM. Another nightly may check the same
# apps too, because no entry pins one. At 0.10.0 the pin was a hard
# requirement (a "roc version mismatch" warning on every app under any other
# nightly), and that is gone.
compiler nightly-2026-10-23-18478ff

# The platform's header pins two packages, and neither is vendored here:
#   roc-lang/http 1.0.0, unchanged since 0.10.0
#   kili-ilo/roc-random 0.9.3 (HoULZB8QzktiNPYSe9myzy9JQzm1nMbjDPVWoA63QpVf.tar.zst),
#     up from 0.9.2. corpus/packages/roc-random/ follows this pin.

index.json <- the release bundle's own .roc modules, parsed by
              `roc-syntax-mcp plugin index`. No source is vendored: the
              manifest names the bundle URL, and every app pins the same one
examples/  <- roc-ray-examples-0.11.1.zip, one directory per example, flattened to
              examples/<name>.roc
topics/    <- this package's own, not upstream's: programs written against
              this pin and declared in plugin.json so they answer by name
overview.md <- this package's own. Its setup, permission and shipping advice
              is drawn from the release notes and docs/*.adoc at this tag, and
              every API claim on it is compiled by verify/overview-snippets.roc

# What examples/ leaves out, and why. In a diff, an omitted example looks the
# same as a missing one, so this list names each omitted example:
#   breakout    imports sibling modules, and the gate compiles one file at a time
#   cave_climb  the same
#   snake       the same
#   top_down    the same
#   live_plot   statically imports a font file the compiler reads at check time
#   roc-doom-e1m1  new in 0.11.0: 26 modules and about 8 MB of Freedoom
#               assets, imported as sibling modules
# breakout, snake and top_down were single files at 0.10.0-rc3. 0.10.0 split
# them into modules.

# What else the release has and this package does not ship:
#   docs/*.adoc  the RocRay manual. It is AsciiDoc, and this host serves only
#                Markdown from a `docs` directory, so to bundle it, this
#                package would have to convert it
#   roc-ray-manual-0.11.1.*, roc-ray-api-docs-0.11.1.zip  rendered copies of
#                the same manual and of the API this corpus indexes

# The repin changes no vendored app. 0.11.0 dropped the `roc:` entry from every
# example header and the release zip already pins the 0.11.1 bundle URL, so
# examples/ is a flat copy of each example's main.roc. (At 0.10.0 the repin
# removed `roc: "nightly-2026-09-27-a3ce7f1"` from each header.) The topics,
# scaffold and verify/ were reformatted with `roc fmt` under the compiler line
# above, as upstream's examples are: it parenthesizes the condition of a
# braceless `if` and expands a call whose last argument is a multi-line closure.
#
# After the repin, apply the patch series to examples/:
#   patch -p1 < patches/0001-load-assets-beside-the-executable.patch
# Upstream's sprite_and_sound and post_process open their assets at a path
# relative to the working directory. Models copy that path, and a built game
# then fails with NotFound when it starts from another directory. The patch
# makes both examples read `assets/` beside the executable, and the source tree
# only with `--dev`, as topics/ray_project.roc does. Regenerate the patch
# against each new tag. Delete it when upstream's examples read their assets
# beside the executable.

